body:
Context: Usage Insights (route/model cost drivers) needs a stable `route` dimension emitted with SDK calls. This PR adds an ergonomic, non-breaking way to tag requests in the TS SDK by introducing a `route` option on request helpers, which maps to a standardized header (`x-redwood-route`). This is intended for the Console feature shipping under ENG-8432 / ENG-8421.

What changed: - Added `route?: string` to request option types used by `client.chat.completions.create`, `client.responses.create`, `client.embeddings.create`, and rerank helpers (where present). - Plumbed the option through the internal request builder so both standard and streaming requests include the header. - Added basic validation/normalization aligned with the current platform guidance: trim whitespace and enforce max length (128). If the value exceeds the max, we truncate and log a debug-level message when a logger is configured; we do not throw (to avoid breaking production traffic). - Added JSDoc guidance + examples emphasizing stable, low-cardinality names (e.g., "app.search" / "support.reply"), and warning against user IDs / request IDs.

Header behavior: - When `route` is provided, we send `x-redwood-route: <route>` on the outbound request. - When `route` is undefined, no header is sent (no change in behavior).

Examples added: ```ts
import { Redwood } from "@redwood-inference/sdk";

const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY! });

// Chat
await client.chat.completions.create({
  model: "ri/llama-3.1-70b-instruct",
  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Write a short summary." }],
  route: "docs.summarize",
});

// Streaming
const stream = await client.chat.completions.stream({
  model: "ri/llama-3.1-70b-instruct",
  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "Draft a reply." }],
  route: "support.draft_reply",
});
for await (const event of stream) {
  // ...
}
```

Tests: - Added unit tests asserting the header is set for: (1) non-streaming chat completion, (2) streaming chat completion, (3) embeddings request. - Added validation tests for trimming and max length truncation. - Updated snapshot fixtures in the request builder tests to include the header when `route` is passed.

Files / areas touched (high level): - src/client.ts (public helper signatures + option plumbing) - src/core/request.ts (header injection + validation helper) - src/types.ts (request option typing) - test/request-route-header.test.ts (new) - test/stream-route-header.test.ts (new) - README.md (examples + naming guidance)

Notes / follow-ups: - This intentionally does not introduce a global default route at client construction time; per-request tagging is preferred to avoid accidental cardinality blowups. If we want an opt-in default later (e.g., `new Redwood({ defaultRoute: ... })`), we should discuss with Telemetry (Logan/Nadia) and Docs (Wei) first.

Review conversation summary: - Alex Martinez: Requested clarity on whether `route` should be named `routeId` to avoid confusion with HTTP routes. Resolved by keeping `route` (matches Console terminology and other SDKs) and documenting that it is an application-defined identifier, not necessarily a URL path. - Nadia Rahman: Asked that the SDK avoid silently throwing away values. We agreed to trim + truncate only, and to preserve as much of the identifier as possible. - Logan Wright: Confirmed header name should be `x-redwood-route` (consistent with telemetry ingestion). - Wei Chen: Requested that README guidance include explicit anti-patterns ("don’t use userId"). Added a short list.

Checklist: - [x] Unit tests added/updated - [x] Streaming path covered - [x] No breaking API changes - [x] README + JSDoc examples updated - [x] Linked to ENG-8432

CI: - build (pass) - lint (pass) - unit tests (pass) - typecheck (pass)

Co-authored-by: Wei Chen (docs edits only)
